00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.000
Leah: Debugging!

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:03.110
what exactly is meant by debugging?

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.730
Debugging is the process of
identifying and removing existing

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.400
and potential errors or ‘bugs’

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:14.749
in code and debugging and testing
are process which occur hand in hand.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.099
Testing aims to identify what happens
when there are errors in the code.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.970
Debugging aims to locate
and rectify mistakes.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:27.709
Often tests are written to identify
bugs as the program is written.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.260
One way to do this is
by writing unit tests.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.639
So, what is a unit test?

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.790
Jack: A unit test is a way of testing
the smallest piece of code that can

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:39.650
be logically isolated in a system.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.309
Smaller tests give a granular view
of how the code is performing and by

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.860
being small, a test can be run fast.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:51.520
Specific tests are written to test whether
a unit is functioning as it should.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.220
This then allows developers to write
a test script, which is essentially

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.670
a piece of code, separate to the main
program, that runs a specific test,

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.610
for each unit in the main program.

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:08.250
Each unit is tested to see if it runs
as expected based on criteria and

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.159
it then summarises this in a report.

00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:15.820
Each time the program is updated, the
test script will be run to ensure that

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.789
all units are functioning correctly.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.639
This helps to debug the whole
program as new units are added.

00:01:22.469 --> 00:01:25.460
Leah: Now we have discussed
debugging let’s look at an

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.360
example of this using code.

